(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-renal-physiology-1-quiz"]={"title":"Renal I: Fluid Regulation & GFR — Quiz (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The trigone of the bladder is best described as:","choices":["The muscular ring of dense skeletal fibers that forms and controls the external urethral sphincter","The peritoneal fold of visceral fascia that suspends and anchors the bladder to the pelvic side wall","The dome-shaped, most distensible portion of the bladder that expands superiorly as urine gradually fills it","A small triangular area immediately superior to the bladder neck, bounded by the ureteral openings and internal urethral orifice"],"answer":3,"exp":"The trigone is a small, smooth triangular area immediately superior to the bladder neck, with the two ureteral orifices at its upper corners and the internal urethral orifice at its lower point. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"As the ureters enter the bladder, they course through the:","choices":["The bladder neck exclusively, without passing through the detrusor muscle","Detrusor muscle, passing 1–2 cm beneath the bladder mucosa","The external urethral sphincter exclusively, entirely bypassing the detrusor","Trigone mucosa only, without crossing muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ureters course obliquely through the detrusor muscle, passing 1–2 cm beneath the bladder mucosa before opening at the trigone's upper corners. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Why can a single action potential cause the entire detrusor muscle to contract as a unit?","choices":["Low-resistance electrical pathways between fused smooth muscle cells let the action potential spread from cell to cell","It is composed of skeletal muscle fibers under direct, conscious voluntary neural control at all times","The detrusor completely lacks gap junctions, so contraction is instead purely hormonal","Each individual detrusor cell has its own independent nerve fiber firing in synchrony"],"answer":0,"exp":"Detrusor smooth muscle fibers extend in all directions and fuse with one another, with low-resistance electrical pathways between cells, so an action potential spreads throughout the muscle and the whole bladder contracts at once. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"The internal urethral sphincter is formed by:","choices":["Detrusor muscle interlaced with a large amount of elastic tissue at the bladder neck","The pudendal nerve's motor endplate region within the urogenital diaphragm","Voluntary skeletal muscle fibers located at the urogenital triangle","A purely mucosal fold of tissue with no underlying muscular component at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"The internal sphincter is detrusor muscle interlaced with substantial elastic tissue within the bladder neck. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which nerve provides the parasympathetic motor supply to the detrusor muscle and carries sensory fibers detecting bladder wall stretch?","choices":["Pelvic nerve","Pudendal nerve","Hypogastric nerve","Ilioinguinal nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pelvic nerves connect with the spinal cord primarily at S2–S3, carrying sensory fibers that detect bladder wall stretch and parasympathetic motor fibers terminating on ganglion cells that innervate the detrusor. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which nerve carries skeletal motor fibers that allow voluntary control over the external bladder sphincter?","choices":["Pudendal nerve","Pelvic nerve","Hypogastric nerve","Vagus nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pudendal nerve carries skeletal motor fibers innervating the external bladder sphincter, giving voluntary/somatic control over urination. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"The hypogastric nerves, arising from the sympathetic chain at the L2 spinal segment, primarily:","choices":["Provide the only innervation to the external urethral sphincter","Stimulate blood vessels, with some sensory fibers for fullness and pain","Innervate the detrusor muscle's parasympathetic ganglia directly","Carry all somatic sensory input from the bladder mucosa"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hypogastric nerves connect with the L2 spinal segment via the sympathetic chain and mainly stimulate blood vessels, with some sensory fibers contributing to sensations of fullness and pain. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"The micturition reflex is best defined as:","choices":["A purely voluntary skeletal muscle contraction with no spinal cord involvement","A reflex that can only ever inhibit bladder emptying and can never facilitate or enhance it in any way","An autonomic spinal cord reflex that empties the bladder, or if it fails, causes a conscious desire to urinate","A reflex confined entirely to the cerebral cortex, with no spinal component at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"The micturition reflex is a nervous, autonomic spinal cord reflex that empties the bladder; if it fails to do so, it causes a conscious desire to urinate instead. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What triggers the onset of the micturition reflex?","choices":["Bladder wall tension rising above a threshold level as the bladder fills","A fixed time interval elapsing since the previous voiding event","Stretch of the ureteral wall alone, independent of the bladder","A sudden drop in systemic blood pressure sensed by arterial baroreceptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"The bladder fills progressively until wall tension rises above a threshold level, at which point the micturition reflex is triggered to empty the bladder. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Sensory signals from bladder-wall stretch receptors travel to the spinal cord and then reflexively back to the bladder via which nerves?","choices":["Pelvic nerves outbound, hypogastric nerves inbound","Pudendal nerves (both directions)","Hypogastric nerves outbound, pelvic nerves inbound","Pelvic nerves (both directions)"],"answer":3,"exp":"As the bladder fills, stretch receptors send sensory signals to the sacral spinal cord through the pelvic nerves, and the reflex signal passes back to the bladder through the parasympathetic fibers of those same pelvic nerves. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"During a strong micturition contraction, detrusor contraction raises bladder pressure to approximately:","choices":["5–10 mmHg","40–60 mmHg","200 mmHg or higher","100–120 mmHg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Detrusor contraction increases bladder pressure to about 40–60 mmHg, which is the major step in emptying the bladder. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which structures can modulate (inhibit or facilitate) the micturition reflex?","choices":["The adrenal medulla only","The cerebral cortex and brainstem","The pancreatic islet cells","The parathyroid gland tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"The micturition reflex, while a spinal reflex, can be inhibited or facilitated by centers in the cerebral cortex and brainstem. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Once filtrate leaves the collecting ducts as urine, what happens to its composition as it travels through the renal calyces, ureters, and into the bladder?","choices":["It is concentrated further by the ureter's countercurrent mechanism","It is extensively modified by tubular reabsorption","It is diluted substantially by ureteral secretion","It essentially remains unchanged"],"answer":3,"exp":"Fluid flowing out of the collecting ducts essentially remains unchanged in composition as it flows from the renal calyces through the ureters to the bladder. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Glomerular filtration rate (GFR) is defined as:","choices":["The total volume of urine that is ultimately excreted from the bladder each minute","The volume of fluid filtered from the glomerular capillaries into Bowman's capsule each minute","The total volume of blood flowing through both kidneys each minute","The overall rate of tubular reabsorption occurring along the entire nephron"],"answer":1,"exp":"GFR is the rate at which the glomeruli filter blood plasma — effectively the volume of fluid filtered from the glomerular capillaries into Bowman's capsule each minute. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the basic equation for GFR?","choices":["GFR = Renal blood flow ÷ filtration fraction","GFR = Kf × Net Filtration Pressure","GFR = Cardiac output × renal fraction","GFR = Tubular reabsorption − tubular secretion"],"answer":1,"exp":"GFR equals the glomerular filtration coefficient (Kf) multiplied by the net filtration pressure across the glomerular capillaries. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"The glomerular capillary filtration coefficient (Kf) is the product of:","choices":["Renal blood flow rate and afferent arteriole vessel diameter","Glomerular hydrostatic pressure and plasma osmotic pressure","Hydraulic conductivity (permeability) and filtering surface area","Plasma protein concentration and molecular size"],"answer":2,"exp":"Kf is the product of the permeability (hydraulic conductivity) and the filtering surface area of the glomerular capillaries. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Why do glomerular capillaries filter fluid at a much higher rate than most other capillaries in the body?","choices":["They have high glomerular hydrostatic pressure and a large capillary filtration coefficient","They are the only capillary beds in the body containing fenestrations","They have unusually low hydrostatic pressure and an unusually small Kf value","They completely lack any filtration barrier separating blood from filtrate"],"answer":0,"exp":"The combination of a high glomerular hydrostatic pressure and a large Kf gives glomerular capillaries a much higher filtration rate than typical capillaries elsewhere in the body. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"The glomerular filtration barrier is composed of which three layers?","choices":["Endothelium, mesangium, and juxtaglomerular cells","Fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocytes","Mesangium, basement membrane, and tubular epithelium","Endothelium, smooth muscle layers, and adventitial connective tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"The filtration barrier consists of fenestrated endothelial cells, the basement membrane, and epithelial podocytes surrounding the outer surface of the basement membrane. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"The perforations in the glomerular capillary endothelium are called:","choices":["Filtration slits","Pedicels","Fenestrae","Slit diaphragms"],"answer":2,"exp":"The glomerular capillary endothelium has thousands of perforations called fenestrae. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Podocytes contribute to the filtration barrier through their footlike processes (pedicels), which are separated by:","choices":["The surrounding mesangial matrix material","Continuous tight junctions with no gaps","Filtration slits with a thin filtration diaphragm","Endothelial fenestrae pores lining the capillary wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Podocyte pedicels are separated by filtration slits bridged by a thin filtration diaphragm containing pores that allow filtrate through while restricting larger molecules. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which proteins found in the podocyte filtration slit diaphragm help restrict the passage of plasma proteins?","choices":["Aquaporin and uromodulin","Collagen and elastin","Actin and myosin","Nephrin and podocin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Nephrin and podocin are proteins in the slit diaphragm that help restrict the flow of plasma proteins across the filtration barrier. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Mesangial cells are best described as:","choices":["The primary anatomical site where erythropoietin hormone is synthesized and released into the blood","Cells that are NOT part of the filtration barrier, but contain contractile actin/myosin microfilaments that may alter capillary blood flow","A required fourth structural layer that completes and reinforces the glomerular filtration barrier itself","Specialized cells found exclusively within the epithelial walls of the renal collecting duct system"],"answer":1,"exp":"Mesangial cells are not part of the glomerular filtration barrier itself; they produce extracellular matrix for structural support and contain actin/myosin-based microfilaments that contact the basement membrane and may alter blood flow through the capillaries. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A substance with a filterability of 1.0 is filtered:","choices":["As freely as water itself","Only after active transport into the filtrate","75% as rapidly as water","Not at all across the glomerular membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"A filterability of 1.0 means a substance passes across the glomerular membrane as freely as water does. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Net filtration pressure across the glomerular capillaries is the sum of four forces. Which force promotes filtration by pushing fluid out of the glomerular capillary?","choices":["Hydrostatic pressure in Bowman's capsule (PB)","Colloid osmotic pressure of glomerular capillary plasma proteins (πG)","Colloid osmotic pressure of proteins in Bowman's capsule (πB), under normal conditions","Glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG) promotes filtration by pushing fluid out of the glomerular capillaries into Bowman's capsule. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Of the four Starling forces governing GFR, which one is described as the determinant most subject to physiological control?","choices":["Hydrostatic pressure in Bowman's capsule (PB)","Colloid osmotic pressure in Bowman's capsule (πB)","Glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG)","Colloid osmotic pressure of glomerular plasma proteins (πG)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG) is the determinant of GFR most subject to physiological control, since PB normally just changes as a function of GFR rather than driving it. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"In the myogenic mechanism of renal autoregulation, increased arterial pressure leads to:","choices":["No vascular response at all, allowing GFR to rise passively and unchecked","Afferent arteriole stretch, triggering smooth muscle contraction and constriction, stabilizing GFR","Efferent arteriole dilation only, occurring with no afferent arteriolar response","Afferent arteriole relaxation, which further increases GFR beyond normal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Increased arterial pressure stretches the afferent arteriole; this stretch activates smooth muscle contraction, constricting the arteriole and preventing excessive increases in glomerular hydrostatic pressure, which helps maintain a stable GFR. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Tubuloglomerular feedback is mediated by which cells monitoring NaCl delivery to the distal tubule?","choices":["Macula densa cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus","Intercalated cells of the collecting duct","Principal cells of the collecting duct","Podocytes of the glomerular filtration barrier"],"answer":0,"exp":"Macula densa cells, part of the juxtaglomerular apparatus, monitor NaCl delivery in the distal tubule and mediate tubuloglomerular feedback. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"When GFR increases and more NaCl reaches the macula densa, what is the immediate downstream effect on the afferent arteriole?","choices":["No effect occurs at all, since the macula densa only responds to low NaCl delivery","The efferent arteriole, rather than the afferent arteriole, constricts sharply in direct response","Adenosine and ATP are released, causing afferent arteriole constriction, and GFR decreases toward normal","Renin release increases sharply, which then dilates the afferent arteriole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Increased NaCl delivery to the macula densa triggers release of adenosine and ATP, which constrict the afferent arteriole and bring GFR back down toward normal. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Renal autoregulation keeps GFR and renal blood flow relatively constant across approximately what range of mean arterial pressure?","choices":["180–250 mmHg","There is no defined range — autoregulation works at all pressures","40–80 mmHg","80–180 mmHg"],"answer":3,"exp":"Renal autoregulation maintains a relatively constant GFR and renal blood flow across arterial pressures of approximately 80–180 mmHg. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"According to this lecture, which factor contributes to average GFR being about 10% lower in women than in men?","choices":["Differences in sex hormone receptor density on podocytes","Women have a structurally different filtration barrier composition","Sex itself, alongside body size, is listed as a factor affecting GFR","GFR does not actually differ by sex once body size is accounted for"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture lists sex — alongside body size — among the factors that affect GFR, noting that average GFR is about 10% lower in women than in men. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
